WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.019
Your accounts don't fit on one machine, so you spread them over four nodes.

2
00:00:07.019 --> 00:00:10.244
Every client must work out on its own where a key lives.

3
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:16.453
The simplest rule: hash the key to a number, and divide by the number of nodes.

4
00:00:16.453 --> 00:00:18.424
The remainder is the node.

5
00:00:18.424 --> 00:00:21.985
A-1001 hashes to 13, and 13 mod 4 is 1: node 1.

6
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.921
Here are twenty keys, hashes zero to nineteen, tinted to match their node.

7
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.785
Now add a fifth node.

8
00:00:28.785 --> 00:00:30.987
The rule becomes hash mod 5.

9
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.832
Only keys zero to three stay put.

10
00:00:33.832 --> 00:00:37.741
Sixteen of twenty move, 80% of your data at once.

11
00:00:38.567 --> 00:00:40.556
Consistent hashing avoids this.

12
00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:43.892
Bend the hash values into a circle, called the ring.

13
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.554
Each node sits on the ring at the hash of its name.

14
00:00:47.554 --> 00:00:49.180
That point is its token.

15
00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.567
A key belongs to the first node clockwise from it.

16
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.260
A-1001 belongs to node 3.

17
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:59.033
A fifth node, node 4, now lands between node 0 and node 1.

18
00:00:59.033 --> 00:01:02.608
It takes only the keys on its arc, all from node 1.

19
00:01:02.608 --> 00:01:04.992
Four keys move instead of sixteen.

20
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.774
But a few random points cut the ring unevenly.

21
00:01:08.774 --> 00:01:12.659
Node 0 holds 30% of the keys, node 1 only fifteen.

22
00:01:12.867 --> 00:01:16.285
So each node takes many tokens, called virtual nodes.

23
00:01:16.285 --> 00:01:19.381
Here it's four each, and Cassandra uses sixteen.

24
00:01:19.381 --> 00:01:23.639
The short arcs average out, and every node holds close to a fifth.

25
00:01:23.867 --> 00:01:27.333
One problem no ring can fix: a hot key.

26
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:34.355
On Black Friday, merchant M-0042 takes 4,000 payments a second, all on one key.

27
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:39.181
A key hashes to a single point, so all of that traffic lands on one node.

28
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.403
To spread it, change the key.

29
00:01:41.403 --> 00:01:45.963
Add a suffix from zero to nine, and the writes land on ten points.

30
00:01:45.963 --> 00:01:47.207
Reads ask all ten.

31
00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:52.771
The ring moves few keys when nodes change, and virtual nodes keep the shares even.

32
00:01:52.771 --> 00:01:54.593
A hot key is yours to split.
